\date{2025-11-02T23:52:21Z}
\title{Motivation}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{Currently, [Swarm](000O) has a relatively standard Hindley-Milner type system, with an extra \em{requirements analysis} bolted on top.  The goal of the requirements analysis is to decide what [capabilities](001D) (and hence what [devices](001E)) are required to execute some code, and to facilitate automatically installing necessary devices on newly built robots.}

\p{However, the bolted-on requirements analysis is fundamentally broken, and has led to a number of bugs. For example, see the following issues:
\ul{
  \li{[Umbrella issue](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/231)}
  \li{[Wrong requirements for `let`-bound variables](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/1466)}
  \li{[Wrong requirements context to `build`](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/1916)}
  \li{[Using recursive functions doesn't require proper capabilities](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/2627)}
  \li{[Making `dictionary` devices more meaningful](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/2151)}
  \li{[Automatic equipment requirement checks don't work when using functions from a nested import](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/2726)}
}
}

\p{Fundamentally, the problem is that a proper requirements analysis must be done statically (requirements analysis for programs passed to the \tt{build} command are currently done \em{dynamically}, at runtime, leading to a number of bugs), and can't be separated from type analysis (\em{e.g.} to be able to deal properly with requirements of higher-order functions).}

\p{The goal of this work is to design a unified type-and-requirements system that handles things properly, using the lessons learned over the past few years of struggling with the current implementation.}
